---
/**
 * Original BotPlanet explainer — the part of the pane a window robot misses.
 *
 * THE ONE HONEST FACT NO MANUFACTURER DRAWS. Every page in this cluster says
 * the same thing in words — "the middle comes up well, the border comes up
 * worst, and the last centimetre against the frame is where a cloth still beats
 * a robot" — and a reader who has only seen the marketing has a picture in their
 * head of a pane cleaned corner to corner. Words fight that picture badly. A
 * picture beats it.
 *
 * The geometry is the reason, not a defect: the machine travels in a path and
 * turns before it reaches the frame, so the border gets the least contact and
 * the corners get least of all. Drawing the turn makes the band obvious without
 * anyone having to accept a claim.
 *
 * NO WIDTH IS PRINTED ON THE BAND. How wide the untouched border actually is
 * varies by machine and by how it turns, and nobody publishes it — putting a
 * figure here would invent a measurement to make a drawing tidier. The band is
 * shown as a band. Conceptual: no product is depicted and no test is implied.
 */
---
<figure class="dgm">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 640 330" width="100%" role="img"
    aria-label="A window pane showing three areas: the centre, which a robot cleans well; a border band, which gets the least contact because the machine turns before the frame; and the corners, which get least of all. A second panel shows that a robot navigating by feel needs a frame, and drives off the edge of frameless glass.">
    <rect x="0" y="0" width="640" height="330" rx="14" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--border)"/>

    <text x="24" y="34" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#f5f5f7">What a window robot actually reaches</text>
    <text x="24" y="54" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">It turns before the frame, so the border gets the least contact. Every brand, every price.</text>

    <!-- ============ left: the pane ============ -->
    <!-- frame -->
    <rect x="30" y="76" width="290" height="212" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="#5a6478" stroke-width="9"/>
    <!-- glass -->
    <rect x="38" y="84" width="274" height="196" fill="rgba(124,193,255,0.10)"/>

    <!-- the border band: what gets least contact -->
    <path d="M38 84 h274 v196 h-274 z M74 120 h202 v124 h-202 z"
      fill="rgba(255,143,107,0.22)" fill-rule="evenodd"/>
    <!-- the corners: least of all -->
    <path d="M38 84 h36 v36 h-36 z" fill="rgba(255,143,107,0.45)"/>
    <path d="M276 84 h36 v36 h-36 z" fill="rgba(255,143,107,0.45)"/>
    <path d="M38 244 h36 v36 h-36 z" fill="rgba(255,143,107,0.45)"/>
    <path d="M276 244 h36 v36 h-36 z" fill="rgba(255,143,107,0.45)"/>

    <!-- the centre, cleaned well -->
    <rect x="74" y="120" width="202" height="124" fill="rgba(79,209,197,0.16)"/>

    <!-- the travelled path, turning short of the frame -->
    <path d="M86 134 H264 M264 134 V158 M264 158 H86 M86 158 V182 M86 182 H264 M264 182 V206 M264 206 H86 M86 206 V230 M86 230 H264"
      fill="none" stroke="#4fd1c5" stroke-width="1.6" opacity="0.75"/>

    {/* A LEGEND UNDER THE PANE, NOT A LABEL INSIDE IT. "Comes up well" was set
        across the middle of the glass and the travel path ran straight through
        it — the drawing struck out its own caption. Naming both zones the same
        way also makes them comparable, which one floating label did not. */}
    <g>
      <rect x="30" y="298" width="12" height="12" rx="3" fill="rgba(79,209,197,0.55)"/>
      <text x="50" y="308" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">Centre — comes up well</text>
      <rect x="196" y="298" width="12" height="12" rx="3" fill="rgba(255,143,107,0.55)"/>
      <text x="216" y="308" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">Border and corners — least contact</text>
    </g>

    <!-- ============ right: the frameless problem ============ -->
    <g>
      <text x="356" y="96" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#f5f5f7">Two limits worth knowing first</text>

      <!-- frameless -->
      <rect x="356" y="112" width="256" height="88" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="370" y="134" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#f0b429">No frame to feel for</text>
      <text x="370" y="153" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">A machine that navigates by finding</text>
      <text x="370" y="168" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">the frame drives off the edge of</text>
      <text x="370" y="183" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">frameless glass. Silence is a no.</text>

      <!-- one side only -->
      <rect x="356" y="210" width="256" height="88" rx="8" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="370" y="232" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#7c93ff">One face at a time</text>
      <text x="370" y="251" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">It cleans the side you place it on.</text>
      <text x="370" y="266" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">An upstairs outside pane is only</text>
      <text x="370" y="281" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">reachable if the window opens in.</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="note">
    Buy one to stop doing the whole window, not to stop doing windows. The border is a consequence
    of how the machine turns rather than a fault in any particular model, and nothing on the market
    eliminates it — a machine built for edges narrows the band, it does not close it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .dgm { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .dgm svg { max-width: 640px; display: block; }
  .dgm .note { margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
